  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       ELEMENT 6 — COMMON PITFALLS
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section style="margin-block: var(--space-10);" aria-labelledby="pitfalls-heading">
    <h2 id="pitfalls-heading" style="font-size: var(--text-2xl); color: #0D2137; margin-bottom: 1rem;">
      Common Extra Help Mistakes and How to Avoid Them
    </h2>

    <h3 style="font-size: 1.15rem; color: #0A3D3A; margin-top: 1rem;">1. Counting Your Primary Home or Vehicle as an Asset</h3>
    <p style="font-size: 1rem; color: #0D2137; line-height: 1.6;">
      Many seniors assume they cannot qualify for Extra Help because they own a home or a car. Social Security explicitly excludes your primary residence, one automobile, household furnishings, personal jewelry, and life insurance policies under $1,500 cash value from the resource test.
    </p>
    <p style="font-size: 1rem; color: #0D2137; line-height: 1.6;">
      <strong>The fix:</strong> Only count liquid savings — bank accounts, stocks, bonds, IRAs, and cash. Verify asset exclusion rules at <a href="https://www.ssa.gov/extrahelp" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" style="color: #0A3D3A;">ssa.gov/extrahelp</a>.
    </p>

    <h3 style="font-size: 1.15rem; color: #0A3D3A; margin-top: 1.25rem;">2. Forgetting to Deduct the $1,500/Person Burial Fund Allowance</h3>
    <p style="font-size: 1rem; color: #0D2137; line-height: 1.6;">
      Social Security automatically deducts $1,500 per person ($3,000 for married couples) from your total savings if you state that the funds will be used for burial expenses. Forgetting to claim this deduction can cause applicants who are slightly over the asset threshold to be denied unnecessarily.
    </p>
    <p style="font-size: 1rem; color: #0D2137; line-height: 1.6;">
      <strong>The fix:</strong> Always check the burial fund intention box on your Extra Help application to ensure the $1,500 per person resource exclusion is applied.
    </p>

    <h3 style="font-size: 1.15rem; color: #0A3D3A; margin-top: 1.25rem;">3. Assuming You Don't Qualify If You Were Denied in Past Years</h3>
    <p style="font-size: 1rem; color: #0D2137; line-height: 1.6;">
      The Inflation Reduction Act expanded Extra Help eligibility starting in 2024 by eliminating Partial Extra Help and making 100% of beneficiaries earning up to 150% FPL eligible for Full Extra Help. If you were denied prior to 2024, you may now qualify.
    </p>
    <p style="font-size: 1rem; color: #0D2137; line-height: 1.6;">
      <strong>The fix:</strong> Re-apply for Extra Help at <a href="https://www.ssa.gov/extrahelp" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" style="color: #0A3D3A;">ssa.gov/extrahelp</a> under the expanded 2026 rules.
    </p>
  </section>

